  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1033 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe long term forecast will start off with a couple more days of \nwarm temperatures and dry conditions. Upper ridging will persist \noverhead through the weekend. This will keep temperatures in the 80s \nto 90s everyday. At the same time, the upper trough off the coast of \nCalifornia will get absorbed into northerly flow as a secondary upper \ntrough tracks south along the western coast. Concurrently, an upper \nlow will develop just east of Texas and shift northeast towards New \nEngland. As these upper systems progress, upper ridging will remain \nsandwiched between the two upper systems sitting just over the \nregion. By early next week, the upper trough off the western coast \nwill move onshore across SoCal into the Desert Southwest. This will \nshift flow aloft to the southwest, ushering in plenty of subtropical \nmoisture. Previous model runs indicated a tropical storm would move \nup the coast of Mexico, aiding in the influx of moisture to our \nregion, however models have backed off on that progression \ncompletely. The increase in moisture and multiple passing shortwaves \nwill bring daily precipitation chances beginning Tuesday. Currently, \nshower and thunderstorm chances remain on the Caprock on Tuesday and \nbecome more widespread through the rest of the week. With cloudy \nskies and daily precipitation chances, we will also see a cool down \nwith highs reaching as low as lower 70s across the far southwestern \nTexas Panhandle.",
